<section image-layout="blank" id="mxvvgsosj88b1mz"><div class="body"><h1 align="center">2026 Market Update</h1><p align="center">Redpoint Ventures — Spring 2026</p><smart-layout variant="solidJoinedBoxes" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>Public Markets</h4><p>Software -20% YTD; SaaS multiples near all-time lows at 4.1x NTM</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>AI CapEx</h4><p>Hyperscaler spend surging to $779B by 2027E — a 36% CAGR</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Private Markets</h4><p>Seed medians at $60M; 24 private $1B+ deals vs. 7 IPOs in 2025</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>CIO Behavior</h4><p>54% consolidating vendors; 45% of AI budgets replacing existing software</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><aside><p>This deck is designed to be read, not presented. Each section contains dense, data-driven analysis with direct implications for founders and operators across hiring, pricing, contracting, and strategic focus.</p></aside></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="f0577xyw5ttgbsi"><div class="body"><h1>We're Living Through a Genuine Technological Revolution</h1><p>The scale and speed of AI investment has no modern precedent — and the fundamentals back it up.</p><columns col-widths="[]"><div background-color="#1B1B27"><h4>CapEx Trajectory</h4><p>Hyperscaler CapEx is surging from <b>$309B in 2024</b> to <b>$779B by 2027E</b> — a <b>36% CAGR</b>. Meta leads at 54% CAGR, Google at 49%, Microsoft at 33%. On the bull case, AI infrastructure spending reaches <b>~3.3% of US GDP by 2030</b>, rivaling the railroad and telecom buildouts of prior centuries.</p><p>2025E: $498B → 2026E: $765B → 2027E: $779B</p></div><div><h4>This Is Not Dotcom 2.0</h4><ul><li>OpenAI and Anthropic each generating <b>$20B+ ARR</b> — real revenue, not speculation</li><li><b>&gt;90%</b> of new data center capacity is pre-committed before completion</li><li>Nvidia EPS at <b>~$4.06</b> vs. Cisco's <b>~$0.37</b> at the 2000 peak — a 10x earnings difference</li><li><b>1B+ monthly active users</b> ~4 years post-ChatGPT vs. only 70M internet users ~4 years post-Netscape</li></ul></div></columns><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/ZE9UqtevDnVPzMUFucU2W.png" source="image.custom"><aside variant="success"><p><b>Founder implication:</b> The infrastructure bet is being made regardless of startup outcomes. Your job is to build the application layer that captures the value this investment creates — not to bet on whether the investment happens.</p></aside></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="iz223umyqvfyffi"><div class="body"><h1>Awe-Inspiring Products Are Shipping Weekly</h1><p>The pace of capability releases has accelerated to a cadence that even practitioners struggle to track. Each breakthrough reshapes the competitive surface for software companies.</p><smart-layout variant="timeline" orientation="landscape" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>Jan '25 — DeepSeek R1</h4><p>Crashed Nvidia stock ~17% in a single session; proved frontier-quality reasoning at a fraction of the cost. Frontier capability is no longer a moat for incumbents.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Apr '25 — Vibe Coding Mainstream</h4><p>Lovable, Replit, Cursor enable non-technical users to ship full apps. Build cost: <b>$500K / 6 months (2015) → $50 / 6 hours (2026)</b>. A 10,000x cost compression in a decade.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>May–Jul '25 — Science Milestones</h4><p>AlphaFold 3 designs novel protein binders for new drugs. OpenAI &amp; Google achieve IMO Gold — beating the world's top mathematicians on competition math.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Feb '26 — Waymo + Frontier Models</h4><p>Waymo operating in 10 US cities with 200M fully autonomous miles driven. Gemini 3.1 &amp; Claude 4.6 deliver reasoning and agentic breakthroughs.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/N6cyUnm9MCYPZGB6C5ndH.png" source="image.custom" align="center"><h4>Developer Demand: Not Falling</h4><p>The narrative that AI kills software jobs is empirically wrong — at least so far. Indeed job postings for software engineers recovered to <b>97%</b> of their Jan 2024 baseline by Jan 2026. Total job postings across all categories sat at only <b>85%</b>. Engineers are not being replaced; they are being levered up.</p><aside variant="info"><p><b>Implication:</b> Hiring strong engineers remains both possible and necessary. The risk is not replacement — it's being outcompeted by a smaller team using better tools.</p></aside></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="4z5n9kxmwb2dr94"><div class="body"><h1>Agents Are Still Early — But the TAM Unlocks Are Enormous</h1><p>The market is largely at the <b>Task Agent</b> stage: AI executes discrete tasks end-to-end with human oversight, operating with an independent runtime of minutes. The unlock from each maturity level is non-linear and massive.</p><infographic prompt="Four maturity levels of AI agents and their addressable market unlock: Level 1 Copilots unlock ~$0.5T US software spend; Level 2 Task Agents unlock ~$1.2T adding $0.7T services automation; Level 3 Workflow Agents unlock ~$2.8T adding $1.6T operational payroll; Level 4 Autonomous Agents unlock ~$6.1T+ adding $3.2T knowledge payroll" layout="staircase" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/p7r4it3una5hm8z/generated-images/S7KHoYLbKQU67zN2.png"></infographic><columns col-widths="[]"><div background-color="#1B1B27"><h4>Coding Was the First Domino</h4><p>GitHub code YoY growth hit <b>~35%</b> in 2025. New iOS apps up <b>~45% YoY</b>. New websites up <b>~32% YoY</b>. AI is <b>expanding</b> software creation, not contracting it — the supply-side constraint on software is dissolving.</p></div><div><h4>What Comes Next</h4><p>Mark Zuckerberg: <i>"Every engineer is effectively gonna end up being more of like a tech lead in the future that has their own little army of engineering agents."</i></p><p>The transition from Task Agents to Workflow Agents adds <b>$1.6T</b> of operational payroll to the addressable market. Companies that own the workflow layer — not just the task layer — capture disproportionate value.</p></div></columns><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/3jnHCsDFwRjsJDrh7fitP.png" source="image.custom"></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="9qn38gca91d3a1b"><div class="body"><h1>Software Stocks Are Down 20% YTD — But the Selloff Is Highly Uneven</h1><p>The headline number masks a story of bifurcation. The market is not selling "software" — it is selling a specific <i>type</i> of software while rewarding another.</p><columns col-widths="[]"><div><h4>2026 YTD Sector Performance</h4><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="sector" label="Sector" type="label"></property><property key="return" label="YTD Return (%)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Energy</cell><cell>32</cell></row><row><cell>S&amp;P 500</cell><cell>-5</cell></row><row><cell>Software</cell><cell>-20</cell></row></data></graph></div><div><h4>Software Sub-Sector Divergence (LTM)</h4><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" y-axis-min="auto" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="subsector" label="Sub-Sector" type="label"></property><property key="return" label="LTM Return (%)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Vertical SaaS</cell><cell>3</cell></row><row><cell>Infrastructure</cell><cell>2</cell></row><row><cell>Horizontal SaaS</cell><cell>-35</cell></row></data></graph></div></columns><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/wWckI890ODA5f8IbTfJWF.png" source="image.custom"><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxes" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>Valuation Floor</h4><p>Public SaaS median NTM revenue multiple at <b>4.1x</b> — near all-time lows, down from a peak of <b>22x</b> in late 2020. The market has repriced the category entirely.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Volatility Signal</h4><p>Lowest-multiple names carry the highest daily volatility (R² = 0.50). The market is punishing <b>uncertainty</b> about business models, not just near-term weakness.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Growth Premium</h4><p>Companies with <b>&gt;20% NTM growth</b> trade at <b>8.1x</b> vs. only <b>2.9x</b> for &lt;10% growers — a nearly 3x multiple gap for demonstrating momentum.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="nrmh2wqts3imqey"><div class="body"><h1>Why Horizontal SaaS Is Getting Hit Hardest</h1><p>The divergence across software sub-sectors reflects a structural logic, not random sentiment. The market is making an explicit judgment about which software products can defend long-term terminal value.</p><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>Vertical SaaS +3%</h4><p>Owns <b>irreplaceable moats</b>: proprietary industry data, compliance workflows, and years of accumulated process history. AI can augment but cannot displace without re-acquiring that data. Switching cost is <b>existential</b>, not cosmetic.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Infrastructure +2%</h4><p>AI is a <b>direct tailwind</b>. New AI workloads run on Snowflake, Datadog, MongoDB, Cloudflare. More AI deployment = more compute, data storage, and observability spend. These companies benefit from every AI dollar deployed.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Horizontal SaaS -35%</h4><p><b>Accidentally optimized for replaceability</b>: designed to serve every industry equally, integrating deeply with none. Without proprietary data or long process history, switching is easier. These products track who does what and when — exactly what AI handles well.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/0ZENNVBzxXnKjBKg3SmMy.png" source="image.custom" align="center"><h4>The Terminal Value Problem</h4><p><b>85–95%</b> of SaaS enterprise value comes from terminal value. A strong quarter moves less than <b>5%</b> of the stock. Investors are pricing in near-zero long-term growth.</p><p>For a representative <b>$100B company</b>: the implied terminal growth rate dropped from <b>4.7% → 1.1%</b> in a single quarter, compressing market cap to <b>$73B</b>. That is not an earnings miss — it is a structural repricing of the business model's long-term defensibility.</p></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="bxjy67kgm01ot9o"><div class="body"><h1>CIOs Are Actively Consolidating Vendors — Demand &amp; Retention at Risk</h1><p>Enterprise software procurement is in active disruption. CIO survey data reveals a buyer base that is simultaneously cutting vendors, shifting budgets toward AI, and renegotiating pricing expectations.</p><columns col-widths="[]"><div><h4>Vendor Consolidation Pressure</h4><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item label="54%"><h4>Consolidating Vendors</h4><p>CIOs actively pursuing vendor consolidation right now</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="45%"><h4>AI Replacing Software</h4><p>Of AI budgets are replacing — not supplementing — existing software budgets</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="3%"><h4>Expect More Vendors</h4><p>Only 3% of CIOs expect AI to lead to more software vendors. The default assumption is fewer.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div><div><h4>Categories Most at Risk of Replacement</h4><p>% of CIOs seriously considering switching in the last year:</p><graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="category" label="Category" type="label"></property><property key="pct" label="% CIOs Considering Switch" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Customer Service Mgmt</cell><cell>26</cell></row><row><cell>Finance Ops</cell><cell>21</cell></row><row><cell>Project Management</cell><cell>20</cell></row><row><cell>Salesforce Automation</cell><cell>19</cell></row><row><cell>HRIS</cell><cell>17</cell></row></data></graph></div></columns><columns col-widths="[35,65]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/8RlYO3ElzKLDvJYdW3cRS.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p></p></div></columns><columns col-widths="[]"><div background-color="#1B1B27"><h4>Pricing Model Shift</h4><p><b>46%</b> of CIOs expect usage/outcome-based pricing to become more common. <b>35%</b> expect flat subscriptions to decline. The enterprise is signaling what it wants — founders who get ahead of this shift will have an advantage in competitive deals.</p></div><div><h4>AI as Spend Driver</h4><p>AI feature additions are cited by <b>58%</b> of CIOs as a key driver of software spend increases — the highest of any category. Products that embed AI meaningfully are growing spend; products without a credible AI story are at risk of consolidation.</p></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="h36lzik54v5mc0v"><div class="body"><h1>AI-Native Companies Are Growing at an Unprecedented Pace</h1><p>The structural efficiency advantage of AI-native companies is compounding. The metrics are not incremental improvements on legacy SaaS benchmarks — they represent a different category of business entirely.</p><columns col-widths="[]"><div><h4>Revenue Scale</h4><p>OpenAI: <b>$25B current revenue run-rate</b>, projecting continued aggressive growth through 2028E. Anthropic: <b>$19B current run-rate</b>. These are real revenue numbers at a scale that took Salesforce over a decade to reach — achieved in years, not decades.</p><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/xFqfSG07ZT7NxE8Z4aLa-.png" source="image.custom"></div><div><h4>Funding Concentration</h4><graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="year" label="Year" type="label"></property><property key="pct" label="Top 20 Deals % of VC Funding" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>2020</cell><cell>8</cell></row><row><cell>2021</cell><cell>6</cell></row><row><cell>2022</cell><cell>7</cell></row><row><cell>2023</cell><cell>23</cell></row><row><cell>2024</cell><cell>31</cell></row><row><cell>2025</cell><cell>44</cell></row></data></graph><p>Top 20 deals captured <b>44%</b> of all enterprise software VC funding in 2025, up from just 8% in 2020.</p></div></columns><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>AI-Native Funding Share</h4><p>VC funding is <b>98% AI-native</b> by Q1 2026, up from ~35% in Q1 2020. Non-AI companies represent only 2% of new enterprise software investment.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Model Labs Outsized Capture</h4><p>Model Labs (OpenAI, Anthropic, xAI) represent only <b>3% of deal count</b> but capture <b>67% of dollars raised</b> and <b>66% of enterprise value</b> created in the category.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>ARR/FTE Efficiency</h4><p>AI-native companies are achieving dramatically higher ARR per FTE than traditional SaaS — a structural efficiency advantage that compounds as headcount stays flat and revenue scales.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="wge3bh00bplohmb"><div class="body"><h1>Private Market Valuations Are Surging — Rounds Are Closing Faster</h1><p>The private market is operating in a different regime than public software. Valuations are resetting upward aggressively at every stage, and the pace of deal execution has accelerated materially.</p><columns col-widths="[]"><div><h4>Median Post-Money Valuations by Stage</h4><graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true" dimensions="{&quot;ratio&quot;:0.56}"><properties><property key="stage" label="Stage" type="label"></property><property key="q1_2024" label="Q1 2024 ($M)" type="data"></property><property key="q1_2026" label="Q1 2026 ($M)" type="data"></property></properties><data><row><cell>Seed</cell><cell>20</cell><cell>60</cell></row><row><cell>Series A</cell><cell>60</cell><cell>150</cell></row><row><cell>Series B</cell><cell>180</cell><cell>480</cell></row><row><cell>Series C</cell><cell>450</cell><cell>1000</cell></row></data></graph></div><div background-color="#1B1B27"><h4>Private Is the New Public</h4><p>The late-stage private market has absorbed the liquidity function that IPOs once served:</p><ul><li><b>24</b> private deals &gt;$1B in 2025 vs. only <b>7 IPOs</b></li><li><b>86%</b> of US companies with $100M+ revenue are now private</li><li>Median days between fundraising rounds falling to multi-year lows since mid-2023</li></ul><p>The best companies are not waiting for ideal conditions — they are raising on momentum, compressing timelines, and capturing terms before the window shifts.</p></div></columns><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/252NhEbSP3Be8Tp8zxBRu.png" source="image.custom"><aside variant="info"><p><b>Historical pattern:</b> Companies founded <b>4–6 years after</b> a major platform shift have historically been the largest value creators of that wave. For AI (ChatGPT launched late 2022), that window is <b>2026–2027</b>. We are in it now.</p></aside></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="dpw1vjomp5jymdr"><div class="body"><h1>What This Means for Your Company Right Now</h1><p>Five concrete, actionable implications drawn directly from the data above. These are not abstract observations — they are decisions to make in the next 90 days.</p><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>1. Prove Your Moat Is Real</h4><p>Investors and CIOs are asking the same question: does your product own irreplaceable data, workflow, or compliance history? If yes, make that case loudly and specifically. If not, articulate your switching cost in concrete terms — what would it cost a customer to leave, measured in time, data loss, and process disruption?</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>2. Rethink Pricing Before Your Customers Do</h4><p><b>46%</b> of CIOs expect usage/outcome-based pricing to become more common; <b>35%</b> expect flat subscriptions to decline. The negotiation is coming regardless. Get ahead of it by piloting outcome-based contracts with your most strategic accounts now, on your terms.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>3. AI Is a Feature AND a Threat — Pick a Side</h4><p><b>58%</b> of CIOs cite AI additions as the top driver of software spend increases. But <b>45%</b> of AI budgets are replacing — not supplementing — existing software. Every product is either an AI consolidation winner or a consolidation target. There is no neutral position.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>4. Move with Urgency on Fundraising</h4><p>Rounds are closing faster. Valuations are up sharply at every stage (Seed 3x in 2 years; Series C at $1B median). But capital is concentrating — the top 20 deals captured <b>44%</b> of dollars in 2025. Best terms go to the fastest movers with the clearest story.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>5. 2026–2027 Is the Historical Sweet Spot — Act Like It</h4><p>Every major platform shift (Internet, Mobile, Cloud) produced its biggest winners from companies founded <b>4–6 years in</b>. The AI clock started in late 2022. The window is open now. Companies that move decisively in this window will define the next decade of enterprise software.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="bdph9n2ksbzmgqr"><div class="body"><h1>Sources</h1><smart-layout variant="sideLineText" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>Primary Source</h4><p><a href="#">Redpoint Ventures — 2026 Market Update (Spring 2026)</a></p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>